data.content:
## What Happened

In C# 14 / .NET 10, overload resolution now prefers `MemoryExtensions.Contains<T>(ReadOnlySpan<T>, T)` over `Enumerable.Contains<T>(IEnumerable<T>, T)` for arrays. Since `ReadOnlySpan<T>` is a ref struct, it cannot be represented in expression trees — causing a runtime failure when the expression is built or interpreted.

## Fix

Disambiguate by explicitly calling `Enumerable.Contains` or casting to `IEnumerable<T>`:

```csharp
// Option 1: Explicit static call
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);

// Option 2: Cast away the array type so the span overload isn't preferred
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => ((IEnumerable<int>)array).Contains(num);
```

Either approach forces the compiler to resolve to the `IEnumerable<T>`-based overload, which is expression-tree–compatible.
